WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
"Is Breakfast the Most Important Meal for
Weight Loss or Should It Be Skipped?"

00:00:10.616 --> 00:00:15.360
Breakfast is&nbsp;widely touted as not only
the most important meal of the day

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.840
in general, but specifically
in relation to weight loss.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.380
This is not just a pop culture
prescription from

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:23.640
checkout aisle magazines,

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.420
but an idea put forward by
prestigious institutions such as

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:32.220
Johns Hopkins, NYU, Mayo Clinic, even
the United States Surgeon General.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.511
"Want to trim your waist?"
read a headline

00:00:35.511 --> 00:00:37.140
from the American
Dietetic Association.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.380
"Try eating breakfast,"
referring to breakfast

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.960
as perhaps the best kept
weight-trimming secret.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.360
But is it true?

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.620
The Duke School of Medicine's
health newsletter was skeptical:

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:55.680
"It's always been billed as the most
important meal of the day—until now."

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.360
While it's widely presumed that eating
breakfast protects against obesity,

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:06.420
the belief is held up as a poster child of
biased distortion of the scientific record.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:09.780
No one can argue that
there isn't an association

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.220
between body weight
and breakfast.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:14.520
Studies have shown that obesity
and breakfast skipping tend

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:19.500
to go together beyond a shadow
of a doubt, in fact gratuitously so.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:25.200
By 1998 we already had what might
be considered strong evidence

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.500
of an association between
breakfast skipping and obesity,

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:33.840
but researchers continued to repeat such
studies to the point of ridiculousness.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:39.960
This meta-analysis found that by 2011,
the combined P value had reached 10-42.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:45.180
OK, what does that mean?
Why is that ridiculous?

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:50.520
In science, P value refers to the chance
of getting a result that extreme

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.040
if in fact there really
was no such effect.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:59.760
How small a chance is 10-42?
This is how small that number is.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:02.280
In other words, the probability
that the association between

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.860
obesity and breakfast
skipping was just a fluke

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:10.620
is less than the chances of winning the
lottery, not once but five times in a row,

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:13.500
and then subsequently getting
struck and killed by lightning.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.880
OK, so the association between
breakfast skipping and obesity

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:22.980
is indeed beyond question.
We know the association is true.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.860
People who skip breakfast are
more likely to be overweight;

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.860
that's beyond a shadow of a doubt.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:32.460
The question, though, is whether
that iron-clad relationship

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:37.140
between breakfast skipping and
obesity is actually cause-and-effect.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.620
To illustrate the difference
between correlation and causation,

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:44.100
let me share an example of
the manipulation of science

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.200
by the candy industry.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:49.800
The National Confectioner's
Association had the gall

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.420
to warn parents that restricting candy
may make their children fat.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:03:00.900
They justify this outlandish claim with
this study, that they funded of course,

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.980
that showed that candy consuming
children and adolescents

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:07.740
were significantly less likely
to be overweight and obese.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:12.420
The industry-funded researchers go
on to imply that this exonerates candy.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:14.580
But what's more likely?

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:17.940
That cutting down on
candy led to obesity,

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:21.540
or rather that obesity led to
the cutting down on candy?

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.240
In other words, the lower candy
consumption may reflect

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.540
the consequences of
obesity, not the cause,

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:31.020
as parents of obese
children try to restrict treats.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:35.700
Similarly, the finding that those who
skip breakfast tend to be heavier

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:39.720
is equivalent to saying those who
are heavier tend to skip breakfast.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:43.800
Doesn't it seem more likely
that overweight individuals

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.040
might just be skipping breakfast
in an effort to eat less,

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:51.600
rather than eating fewer meals
somehow leading to weight gain?

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.100
Now it's possible that skipping breakfast
could slow your metabolism

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:01.080
or cause you to overeat so much later
in the day that you'd gain weight,

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:06.000
but you can't know for sure
until you put it to the test.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:11.580
Sometimes, randomized controlled trials
are infeasible, impossible, or unethical.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:15.120
To test to see if parachutes save lives,

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.300
you can't exactly boot half the
people off a plane without them.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:23.700
But you could easily randomize people to
eat breakfast or not to see what happens.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:27.600
And it turns out eating breakfast does
not seem to affect your metabolism rate

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.000
or sufficiently suppress your appetite.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.140
Most studies—95%—found that
eating breakfast tends to lead

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:37.560
to the same or greater
caloric intake over the day.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:41.220
Even when people ate more at
lunch after skipping breakfast,

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:45.240
they didn't tend to eat an entire
breakfast worth of calories more,

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:48.360
and so ended up eating
fewer calories overall.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:51.240
For example, feed people about
a 500-calorie breakfast,

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.420
and at lunch they may eat
about 150 calories less

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:57.840
than those randomized to skip
breakfast, but they would still end up

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:01.980
with about a 350-calorie surplus
over the breakfast skippers.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:06.000
Does this then translate
into weight gain over time?

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:11.520
Researchers at Brigham Young University
randomized 49 women who habitually

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:15.900
skipped breakfast to either start eating
breakfast or continue skipping.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:19.440
If breakfast somehow
magically leads to weight loss,

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:22.560
then the newly eating breakfast
group should benefit.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.280
But no, compared to those who
continued to skip breakfast,

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:30.660
adding the extra meal led to hundreds
more daily calories consumed

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.060
and nearly a half pound
of weight gain a week.

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:38.880
If you already eat breakfast and start
skipping it, will you lose weight?

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.000
We'll find out next.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:47.400
Where did this whole

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:51.240
breakfast-is-the-most-important-meal-
of-the-day concept come from?

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:56.400
The father of public relations,
Edward Bernays, infamous for his

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:00.840
"Torches of Freedom" campaign to get
women to start smoking back in the 1920s,

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:03.540
was paid by a bacon company

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:07.020
to popularize the emblematic
bacon and eggs breakfast.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:11.820
The role of public relations, he wrote
in his book entitled "Propaganda,"

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:16.920
is the conscious and intelligent
manipulation of the organized

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:22.260
habits and opinions of the masses.
Public relations specialists thereby

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:26.940
constitute an invisible government,
the true ruling power of our country.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:33.780
Breakfast is big business. Powerful
corporate interests such as the breakfast

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:38.160
cereal lobby are blamed for perpetuating
myths about the importance of breakfast.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:42.120
This editorial in The American Journal
of Clinical Nutrition urged nutrition

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:46.380
scientists to speak truth to power
and challenge conventional wisdom

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:49.680
when necessary even when
it looks like we are taking away

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:54.120
motherhood and apple pie.
Actually, the editorial concludes,

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:58.680
reducing the portion size of apple pie
might not be a bad idea, either.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:02.460
So, should we break the feast
and skip breakfast to lose weight?

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:06.720
Though advice to eliminate breakfast will
surely pit nutrition scientists against

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:09.720
the very strong and powerful
food industry, skipping breakfast

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:13.500
has been described as a
straightforward and feasible strategy

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:18.300
to reduce daily calorie intake.
Unfortunately, it doesn't seem to work.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:21.780
Most randomized controlled studies
of breakfast skipping found

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:23.820
no weight loss benefit
to omitting breakfast.

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:29.100
How is it possible if skipping breakfast
means skipping calories?

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:34.020
The Bath Breakfast Project—
a famous series of experiments

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:37.800
run not out of the tub, but the
University of Bath in the UK—

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:40.020
discovered a key to the mystery.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:42.660
Men and women randomized
to either eat breakfast—

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:46.260
defined as taking in at least
700 calories before 11am—

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:47.880
or fast until noon every day.

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:52.080
As in similar trials, the breakfast
eating group ate a little less throughout

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:55.920
the rest of the day, but still ended up
with hundreds of excess daily calories

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:57.660
over the breakfast skippers.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.820
Those who ate breakfast consumed
more than 500 calories a day more.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:07.680
I mean, over six weeks that would
add up to over 20,000 extra calories.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:12.840
Yet after six weeks both groups ended up
with the exact same change in body fat.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:17.880
Wait! How could tens of thousands of
calories just effectively disappear?

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:21.600
If more calories were going in
with no change in weight,

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:25.500
then there must have been more
calories going out. And indeed,

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:30.180
the breakfast group was found to
spontaneously engage in more kind

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:32.640
of light-intensity physical
activity in the mornings

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:34.140
than the breakfast skipping group.

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:39.000
Light intensity activities include things
like casual walking, light housecleaning

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:43.320
activities, not structured exercise
per se, but apparently enough

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:47.040
extra activity to use up the bulk
of those excess breakfast calories.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:52.440
There's a popular misconception that our
body goes into energy conservation mode

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:55.020
when we skip breakfast by
slowing our metabolic rate.

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:57.300
That does not appear to be true,

00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:02.400
but maybe our body does intuitively
slow us down in other ways.

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:03.420
When we skip breakfast

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:06.780
our body just doesn't seem to
want to move around as much.

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:11.640
The extra activity didn't completely
make up for the added calories though.

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:16.140
We seem to still be missing
about 100 daily calories,

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:20.700
suggesting there may be another
factor to account for the mystery

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:22.320
of the MIA morning calories.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:25.620
Recent breakthroughs in
the field of chronobiology—

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:31.380
the study of our body's natural rhythms—
have unsettled an even more sacred cow

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:35.220
of nutrition dogma: the concept
that a calorie is a calorie.

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:39.240
It's not just what we eat,
but when we eat.

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:44.880
Same number of calories, different
weight loss, depending on meal timing.

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:49.080
Just to give you a taste, the exact same
number of calories at breakfast are

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:52.740
significantly less fattening than
the same number of calories

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:57.120
eaten at supper. What?
That's just mind-blowing.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:59.700
A diet with a bigger breakfast
causes more weight loss

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:04.560
than the same diet with a bigger dinner.
Because of our circadian rhythms,

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:08.400
morning calories don't appear to
count as much as evening calories.

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:14.700
So maybe breakfast should indeed be the
most important meal of the day after all.

